{"id": 10, "ts": 1780469470.5019557, "event": "public_discussion_message", "round": 1, "agent": "emily_brooks", "message": "I appreciate James\u2019s focus on Candidate\u202fD\u2019s reliability and problem\u2011solving, but the missing evidence on crew cooperation and the new note that D is regarded as arrogant with relatively weak leadership raise serious doubts about his ability to work effectively in a cockpit team. Long\u2011distance flights demand not only technical reliability but also strong, collaborative communication under pressure. Since we have no concrete examples of D\u2019s crew interaction and his interpersonal traits are concerning, I am less convinced that D is the safest overall choice.\nCandidate\u202fB, on the other hand, demonstrates clear strengths across several critical criteria: he is very conscientious (operational reliability), handles stress very well (stress resilience), and excels at assessing weather conditions and computer systems (technical competence). While he is sometimes perceived as pretentious and may adopt the wrong tone, these issues are more limited in scope than D\u2019s broader leadership and cooperation concerns. Given B\u2019s proven stress handling and conscientiousness, I believe he offers a more balanced fit for the long\u2011distance pilot role.\nTherefore, I would shift my provisional vote to **Candidate\u202fB** as the preferred hire, pending any further evidence on crew cooperation for D or additional concerns for B."}
